WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.290
You tell a child not to lie, and the child asks you why.

2
00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.980
Because it's forbidden, you say.

3
00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:15.910
But is it bad because it's forbidden, or forbidden because it's bad — and whichever answer you give changes, from the ground up, what morality rests on.

4
00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:31.600
The porch of the official the Athenians called the king archon was an ordinary stone portico where court business was handled.

5
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:38.770
In the spring of three ninety-nine B.C., a man of seventy was on his way there to register the case that had been brought against him.

6
00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:46.100
A young poet named Meletus had charged him with showing no respect for the gods and with corrupting the minds of the young.

7
00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.550
The man was Socrates, and within a few weeks he would drink hemlock.

8
00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.200
On the steps he met an acquaintance.

9
00:00:54.200 --> 00:01:00.730
Euthyphro was a man who took himself for a seer, mocked in the assemblies for claiming that he could read the future.

10
00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:02.940
He had a case of his own.

11
00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.110
But the defendant was his own father.

12
00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:08.640
The story ran like this.

13
00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:15.010
A day laborer working on family land on the island of Naxos had killed a slave in a drunken quarrel.

14
00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:23.460
Euthyphro's father had the man bound and thrown into a ditch, then sent word to Athens to ask an interpreter of religious law what ought to be done.

15
00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:28.550
Before the messenger returned, the man in the ditch had died of hunger and cold.

16
00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:32.200
Now the son was prosecuting his father for murder.

17
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.690
In Athens this was beyond comprehension.

18
00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:40.460
To bring your own father to court was to trample on the sacred bond of the family.

19
00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:50.030
What was more, the dead man was neither a relative nor a free man; by the common understanding of that age, this was not even a matter in which the son had any standing.

20
00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:56.880
Euthyphro's answer was perfectly clear: what he was doing was pious, it was pleasing to the gods, it was holy.

21
00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:00.850
That is exactly what Socrates fastened onto.

22
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:05.140
If you are so certain, he said, then teach me what holiness is.

23
00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:09.110
Do not give me examples; give me a definition.

24
00:02:09.110 --> 00:02:13.400
Euthyphro's first answer was: that which the gods love.

25
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:23.450
Socrates reminded him that the gods are forever quarreling among themselves, one loving what another hates; in that case the very same act turns out to be both holy and unholy.

26
00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:30.140
So Euthyphro corrected his definition: that which all the gods love.

27
00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:36.510
And when the dialogue reaches its tenth section, perhaps the sharpest question in the history of philosophy is asked.

28
00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:44.000
Socrates asked: is the holy loved by the gods because it is holy, or does it become holy because the gods love it?

29
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.290
Looked at carelessly, that sentence resembles a play on words.

30
00:02:48.290 --> 00:02:50.180
It is not.

31
00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:54.630
It builds two separate worlds, and it leaves no room to stand between them.

32
00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.800
Consider the first possibility.

33
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.650
Goodness exists before the command.

34
00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:05.260
A thing is good in itself, and the gods, being good, love it.

35
00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:10.030
In that case the command is not the cause of goodness but its consequence.

36
00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:14.320
God is like a seal: confirming a truth that is already there.

37
00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.010
Consider the second possibility.

38
00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.700
Goodness is born of the command.

39
00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:23.190
What makes a thing good is that it has been commanded.

40
00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:29.880
Before the command there is no such thing as good or evil; there is only will, and whatever that will commands is the good.

41
00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:36.250
Let us not leave this question on the temple steps, because it meets us inside ordinary life every day.

42
00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:40.060
A child asks their mother: why is lying wrong?

43
00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:42.750
The mother gives one of two answers.

44
00:03:42.750 --> 00:03:52.480
Either she says, because I say so, or she says, because lying destroys people's trust in one another, and you would not want to live in a world where nobody believes you.

45
00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.450
The first answer ties the rule to authority.

46
00:03:56.450 --> 00:04:00.260
The second ties the rule to something deeper than the rule itself.

47
00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:04.070
The same question is asked in a workplace.

48
00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:07.880
The instruction your manager has given strikes you as wrong.

49
00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:14.570
Is the phrase company policy the sentence that ends the discussion, or is it the place where the real discussion begins?

50
00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:21.900
The same question is asked in a courtroom: is a thing right because it is legal, or should it become law because it is right?

51
00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:31.790
The objection voiced by defense counsel at Nuremberg, that they had merely obeyed the orders then in force, is in fact this twenty-four-hundred-year-old question entered into the court record.

52
00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:35.600
When Euthyphro met this question, he did not know what to do.

53
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.370
He circled about for a while, then suddenly said he was in a hurry and left.

54
00:04:40.370 --> 00:04:43.860
The dialogue ends without arriving at any definition.

55
00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.710
But the question did not leave.

56
00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:52.280
It went on its way, crossing the Mediterranean, the centuries, and the religions.

57
00:04:52.280 --> 00:05:00.570
In the eighth and ninth centuries, in the theological assemblies of Basra and Baghdad, the same question began to be asked in another language.

58
00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:08.220
In that language its name was husn and qubh: the question of what determines whether an act is beautiful or ugly, good or evil.

59
00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:14.590
The school of thought known as the Mu'tazila gave a clear answer: reason can know good and evil on its own.

60
00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:19.680
Today that claim may sound harmless, but its consequences were enormous.

61
00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:27.970
For the Mu'tazila, justice did not take its beauty from a command; justice was already beautiful, and that is why it had been commanded.

62
00:05:27.970 --> 00:05:33.860
Oppression was not ugly because it had been forbidden; it had been forbidden because it was ugly.

63
00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:41.350
Qadi Abd al-Jabbar, who lived in the city of Rayy and died in ten twenty-five, is among the most systematic defenders of this view.

64
00:05:41.350 --> 00:05:53.000
An example of the kind he often used ran like this: you need no sacred text at all to understand that it is ugly to repay with harm someone who has never harmed you, someone who has in fact done you good.

65
00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:59.050
Even a person who has never heard a revelation, who grew up alone on a mountainside, knows this.

66
00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:07.980
For the Mu'tazila this knowledge lies ready in the human mind; revelation does not create it, it reminds and completes.

67
00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.670
And what was the price?

68
00:06:10.670 --> 00:06:18.960
The price was this: if justice and oppression are realities independent of the divine will, then God is in a sense subject to them.

69
00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:24.210
The Mu'tazila saw no harm in accepting that; they even counted it as praise.

70
00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:28.020
God cannot do injustice, because God is just.

71
00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.990
But to their opponents this meant placing limits on absolute power.

72
00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:36.120
Was there an uncreated moral law standing above the Creator?

73
00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:43.610
The strongest voice raised against that objection was Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari, who died in nine thirty-six.

74
00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:53.180
What makes it interesting is that al-Ash'ari himself had been raised for many years inside the Mu'tazila; he was the student of the school's great teacher Abu Ali al-Jubba'i.

75
00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:58.910
Then he broke away and founded one of the most widespread schools of theology in the Islamic world.

76
00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:02.080
There is a famous account of how he left.

77
00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.410
He asks his teacher about three brothers.

78
00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:11.460
One lived a virtuous life and died; one died in sin; the third died as a small child.

79
00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:19.750
The teacher says that the first will be rewarded, the second punished, and that the child will be saved but will not reach the rank of the virtuous one.

80
00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:29.160
Al-Ash'ari asks: and if the child says, Lord, why did you take me early, had I lived I too would have been virtuous, what answer is given?

81
00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:35.690
The teacher says, had you lived you would have become a sinner and gone to hell; this was better for you.

82
00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:46.860
Al-Ash'ari makes his final move: in that case, if the sinful brother asks, Lord, since you knew what would become of him and had mercy on him, why did you not take me as a child too?

83
00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:50.670
According to the account, the teacher falls silent.

84
00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:56.720
The historical details of this story are disputed, but the idea it carries is perfectly clear.

85
00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:05.810
The conclusion al-Ash'ari reached was this: if you set out to place divine wisdom on the scales of human justice, you will come away empty-handed every time.

86
00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:09.300
Because those scales are the scales of a human being.

87
00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:17.430
For the Ash'ari school, husn and qubh belong to revealed law: the good is what God commands, the evil what God forbids.

88
00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:20.920
Before the command, acts are morally naked.

89
00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:28.570
Put in the most extreme formulation of this view, if God commanded today the exact opposite of what He has forbidden, that too would be good.

90
00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:33.980
Here the two ends of Socrates' question turned into a tension that would last for centuries.

91
00:08:33.980 --> 00:08:40.190
On one side, a view that makes morality firm and objective but sets a limit on divine power.

92
00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:45.760
On the other, a view that leaves power infinite but binds morality to the discretion of a will.

93
00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:49.570
This tension was not peculiar to the Islamic world.

94
00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:56.100
In the West, in the fourteenth century, the English thinker William of Ockham walked a similar road.

95
00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:00.390
For him too, moral values were the product of the divine will.

96
00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:08.520
God could, if He wished, command that He be hated, and when that command was carried out, a virtuous deed would have been done.

97
00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:17.450
A view of this kind is today called voluntarism, and Ockham, who in all likelihood never read al-Ash'ari, was standing at the edge of the same abyss.

98
00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:20.300
Was there a way of standing in the middle?

99
00:09:20.300 --> 00:09:22.350
There was.

100
00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:29.680
And the man who opened it was Abu Mansur al-Maturidi, born in Maturid near Samarkand, who died in nine forty-four.

101
00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:41.330
This thinker, who flourished in Transoxiana, in the lands of present-day Uzbekistan, and who established one of the main arteries of Turkish-Islamic thought, pointed to the place where both sides are right.

102
00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:46.580
For al-Maturidi, reason really can grasp the good and the evil in acts.

103
00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:52.310
The ugliness of oppression finds an answer in the human mind, and so does the beauty of justice.

104
00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:56.120
Up to here he stands where the Mu'tazila stand.

105
00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:03.930
But he draws a distinction: knowing that a thing is good is one matter, and that thing being bound upon you as an obligation is another.

106
00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:08.540
Knowing belongs to reason; binding force belongs to revelation.

107
00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:13.630
Reason sees the good; revelation turns it into responsibility.

108
00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:19.200
In this way morality is not made arbitrary, and reason does not take the place of revelation.

109
00:10:19.200 --> 00:10:22.370
This may look like a formula for compromise.

110
00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:25.860
But in fact it carries the question to a finer place.

111
00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:31.110
Socrates' question was: what establishes goodness?

112
00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:37.640
Al-Maturidi's distinction adds a second question: is knowing the good the same thing as being obliged to it?

113
00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:43.210
Now let us put weight on the other pan of the scales, because what waits there is not light either.

114
00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:46.860
Suppose you chose the first possibility.

115
00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:51.790
Goodness is good in itself; the command confirms it rather than creating it.

116
00:10:51.790 --> 00:10:55.280
True, this view secures morality.

117
00:10:55.280 --> 00:11:01.010
But a question is left standing: if goodness is already there, what is the command for?

118
00:11:01.010 --> 00:11:04.820
Is God necessary for morality to stay on its feet?

119
00:11:04.820 --> 00:11:09.430
The most famous answer to this question is not really an answer but a fear.

120
00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:21.720
In the novel The Brothers Karamazov, which Dostoevsky published in eighteen eighty, there is an idea that circles around the character of Ivan: if there is no God and no immortality, everything is permitted.

121
00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:28.730
One detail has to be stated correctly here, because this is one of the most misquoted sentences in the world.

122
00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:39.420
In the novel the words do not come out of Ivan's mouth in precisely that mold, like a slogan; the idea enters circulation through other characters reporting it and arguing over it.

123
00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:46.590
The one who sharpened the formula was Sartre, in the famous lecture on existentialism he gave in nineteen forty-six.

124
00:11:46.590 --> 00:11:56.160
Sartre used the sentence not as an accusation but as a starting point: the human being has been abandoned, there is no ready-made table of values to hold on to,

125
00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:01.730
and precisely for that reason each person is personally responsible for everything they do.

126
00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:07.620
But history had long since shown that without God morality collapses is not so easy a claim.

127
00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:14.310
In sixteen twenty-five a Dutch jurist named Hugo Grotius wrote a book on the law of war and peace.

128
00:12:14.310 --> 00:12:21.320
This work, which we now call On the Law of War and Peace, counts among the founding texts of modern international law.

129
00:12:21.320 --> 00:12:31.690
In it Grotius sets out an assumption that was shocking for his age: let us suppose, and it is a great sin even to say it, that God does not exist or takes no interest in human affairs.

130
00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:34.540
The rules of natural law would still hold.

131
00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:44.270
This sentence needs underlining, because Grotius was not an unbeliever; he was a devout Protestant who had been sentenced to life imprisonment in his own country on account of his faith,

132
00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:48.400
and who escaped from the fortress hidden in his wife's book chest.

133
00:12:48.400 --> 00:12:54.610
What he was doing was not setting God aside but showing that morality and law have an inner logic of their own.

134
00:12:54.610 --> 00:13:02.740
Just as two plus two makes four, fidelity to a contract being good and gratuitous cruelty being evil belonged to the nature of things.

135
00:13:02.740 --> 00:13:08.310
On the other hand, a serious objection came to those who chose the side of the command as well.

136
00:13:08.310 --> 00:13:15.640
One of the clearest statements of it is Leibniz's, in a text he wrote on the concept of justice at the beginning of the eighteenth century.

137
00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:21.690
He says: if goodness is merely what God wills, then what does it mean to say that God is good?

138
00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:29.020
That sentence turns into God wills what He wills, and what you are left holding is not praise but an empty tautology.

139
00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:34.110
If you are praising divine justice, what you praise must have a meaning independent of will.

140
00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:37.760
Both sides carry a knife that draws the other's blood.

141
00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:43.650
Choose the side of the command, and morality becomes arbitrary and your praise loses its meaning.

142
00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:50.340
Choose the side of goodness in itself, and morality becomes firm but the place of the command begins to shake.

143
00:13:50.340 --> 00:13:55.750
And this deadlock, abstract as it looks, has a very concrete echo.

144
00:13:55.750 --> 00:14:04.200
What Nietzsche was describing at the end of the nineteenth century when he said that God is dead was not a declaration of victory but the description of a wreck.

145
00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:09.930
He spoke of a sponge wiping away humanity's horizon, of a falling that goes on falling.

146
00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:18.380
His real warning was this: people have stopped believing in God, but they expect the moral demands that came from God to stay perfectly in place.

147
00:14:18.380 --> 00:14:22.990
Something like cutting off the branches and still wanting the root to feed them.

148
00:14:22.990 --> 00:14:27.120
Today a great many people live in this strange middle state.

149
00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:31.570
They no longer know where the rule comes from, but they still feel its weight.

150
00:14:31.570 --> 00:14:40.340
They keep a promise they never made to anyone, they behave honestly where nobody can see them, and they have no ready answer for those who ask why they do it.

151
00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:49.430
This is where the sorrow is to be looked for: the ladder of command has been pulled away, but the person is still standing up there, with no memory of how they climbed.

152
00:14:49.430 --> 00:14:53.080
Socrates' question appears to have two ends.

153
00:14:53.080 --> 00:15:00.730
But as with most dilemmas of this kind, there is a hidden third possibility, and that possibility takes aim at the way the question is built.

154
00:15:00.730 --> 00:15:11.260
In Islamic thought and in Christian thought alike, this road was opened as follows: what if goodness is not an independent law standing above God, but the expression of God's own nature?

155
00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:19.550
Then the command ceases to be an arbitrary decision taken from outside, and goodness itself ceases to be a cold tablet hanging above God.

156
00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:22.880
It becomes something like the sun giving light.

157
00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:28.930
The sun does not decide, now let me give light; light is a consequence of what the sun is.

158
00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:32.900
Whether this answer fully resolves the dilemma is open to argument.

159
00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:39.750
When you say that God's nature is good, it can be objected that you still have to take the measure of that goodness from somewhere.

160
00:15:39.750 --> 00:15:48.200
But it is true that it at least blunts both ends of the dilemma: no arbitrariness is left, and no external law that turns God into a surplus.

161
00:15:48.200 --> 00:15:52.330
Yet at the end of this part there is something else that really needs saying.

162
00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:55.980
Socrates' question asks about the source of goodness.

163
00:15:55.980 --> 00:16:00.430
Life generally asks us a different question: who is a good person?

164
00:16:00.430 --> 00:16:03.440
These two questions are not the same.

165
00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:08.050
Someone who knows the answer to the first perfectly may fail the second.

166
00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:13.300
Consider this: two people find a wallet, and both return it to its owner.

167
00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:16.950
One of them because they saw the security camera.

168
00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:20.440
The other because the camera never crossed their mind.

169
00:16:20.440 --> 00:16:24.890
To someone looking from outside, the two acts are identical.

170
00:16:24.890 --> 00:16:31.580
But these two are not the same person, and getting into the same elevator as the second one is a different matter altogether.

171
00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:36.030
In Islamic moral thought the name of this distinction is intention.

172
00:16:36.030 --> 00:16:47.200
When al-Ghazali explains the difference between the outer shell of acts of worship and deeds and their inner meaning, he places at the center the tradition reporting that deeds are judged according to intentions.

173
00:16:47.200 --> 00:16:51.650
Two hands making the same movement may not be doing the same thing.

174
00:16:51.650 --> 00:16:57.380
The distance between carrying out the rule and being good is a distance the rule itself cannot measure.

175
00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:03.910
Aristotle came at this matter from another direction: virtue is not a piece of knowledge but a habit.

176
00:17:03.910 --> 00:17:09.480
You learn to be brave by acting bravely, just as you learn to play the lyre by playing it.

177
00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:16.330
Knowing by heart what the right action is may be of no use when the frightening moment that calls for that action finally arrives.

178
00:17:16.330 --> 00:17:25.260
Kant, at the end of the eighteenth century, drew the same distinction in his own language: acting in accordance with duty is not the same as acting from duty.

179
00:17:25.260 --> 00:17:33.870
The shopkeeper who does not cheat their customers, if they do it only for the reputation of the shop, is an honest merchant but not yet an honest person.

180
00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:38.000
This distinction catches us today in a place we never expected.

181
00:17:38.000 --> 00:17:42.130
Modern life has largely handed goodness over to rules.

182
00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:47.700
Codes of ethics, compliance units, signature authorities, tick boxes.

183
00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:49.910
All of these are necessary.

184
00:17:49.910 --> 00:17:55.160
But they share one side effect: reducing a person's moral judgment to a checklist.

185
00:17:55.160 --> 00:18:02.010
To say that whatever the regulation does not forbid is permitted is Euthyphro's answer wearing corporate clothes.

186
00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:09.500
That same question is being placed today, in a form harsher than at any point in history, before artificial intelligence systems.

187
00:18:09.500 --> 00:18:12.510
How do you teach a machine to be good?

188
00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:21.760
Give it a list of rules, and it will not know what to do in a situation the list does not cover; it may keep faith with the letter of the rule while trampling its spirit.

189
00:18:21.760 --> 00:18:28.450
And if you set out to give it a power of judgment that goes beyond the list, where will you take the measure of that judgment from?

190
00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:38.980
This is Socrates' question written onto silicon, and at this moment the world's finest engineers stand before it not much more comfortably than Euthyphro did two thousand four hundred years ago.

191
00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:46.310
Perhaps the matter is this: the question of the source of goodness is a question of thought, and its answer takes a long time.

192
00:18:46.310 --> 00:18:51.560
But the question of being good is a question of life, and its answer cannot be postponed.

193
00:18:51.560 --> 00:18:58.410
Without knowing whether the command came first or goodness did, you can still choose not to speak ill of someone behind their back.

194
00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:06.860
Without settling whether husn and qubh are matters of reason or of revealed law, you can still tell the truth at a moment when nobody is watching.

195
00:19:06.860 --> 00:19:11.310
That day on the steps, Euthyphro said he was in a hurry and left.

196
00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:16.400
For hundreds of years, everyone who reads that dialogue has thought that he ran away.

197
00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:21.010
But perhaps the real flight is not leaving without being able to answer the question.

198
00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:26.100
The real flight was believing you knew the answer and forgetting the man in the ditch.

199
00:19:26.100 --> 00:19:35.030
Because at the place where that whole argument began, a human being whose name we do not even know had died, bound, waiting for someone who never came back.

200
00:19:35.030 --> 00:19:41.240
However elegant Socrates' question may be on paper, there is always a ditch like that behind it.

201
00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:45.050
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

202
00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:49.020
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
